   "text": "# portlog\n\n## What was on port 3000 an hour ago?\n\nportlog records which process held each local TCP port over time, so you can look it up after the process has exited.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nRuns on macOS and Linux. Written in Go. MIT licensed.\n\n## Start recording\n\n```sh\nportlog start\n```\n\nRuns a background recorder. History is kept for 7 days in `~/.portlog/`.\n\nTo stop recording:\n\n```sh\nportlog stop\n```\n\n## Look up a port\n\nSee every process that held port 3000, newest first:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000\n```\n\nLimit the results to the last two hours:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000 --since 2h\n```\n\n## Free a port\n\nStop the process holding port 3000 now, after confirmation:\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000\n```\n\nSkip the confirmation with `-y`:\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000 -y\n```\n\n## What gets recorded\n\nportlog records local TCP port ownership. It never records command-line arguments, because they can hold secrets.",
